  <div class="container">
    <h2 id="name-title">How the neighborhood got its name</h2>
    {# Text carried over from the old About page. TODO: the population and household
       figures came from that page and should be checked against current census data. #}
    <p>The Kennedy-King neighborhood dates back to the 1800s and is rich in architectural history. The name Kennedy-King, though, came about on April 4, 1968, with the assassination of Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.</p>
    <p>On that cold and dreary April evening, Robert Kennedy was scheduled to give a campaign speech in Indianapolis. Instead, he delivered a word of encouragement to a city broken by devastating news. A historic landmark was created that night, and a great neighborhood was given its name.</p>
    <p>Kennedy-King is one of Indianapolis's fastest growing urban neighborhoods. With a population under a thousand and fewer than four hundred households, it's a quiet and unique neighborhood to be part of.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--alt" aria-labelledby="speech-title">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 id="speech-title">April 4, 1968</h2>
    <p>On April 4, 1968, Robert F. Kennedy announced the death of Martin Luther King Jr. to a mostly Black crowd in an Indianapolis park. His impromptu speech is regarded as one of the most powerful and important addresses in American history.</p>
    <p>Kennedy's words were equal parts eulogy and call for peace, and they came as violence erupted across America. Because of his message of compassion, the crowd in Indianapolis went home peacefully. As cities across the country burned, Indianapolis remained calm.</p>

